A||||4||Gold87SE||Overheating||||1||
Z||000000||Gold87SE||12-18-2000||01:09 AM||||My car has been overheating and there are no leaks. One thing I noticed is that when I shut the car off, there is a whining type noise that sounds like it's coming from the fan area and it sounds for about 20 seconds. If I turn the fan on when it's making this noise it goes away and then when I shut it back off it comes back. Does anyone know what this could be and if it could be linked to the overheating?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||12-18-2000||09:21 AM||||The whinning you speak of I feel is the radiator cap venting pressure. Have you replaced the cap?<BR>Full of coolant and do you get heat? Good thermostat installed?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Gold87SE||12-18-2000||01:00 PM||||I tried 3 thermostats and the coolant keeps boiling off so i go through it pretty quick. The engine is new and has less than 3,000 miles on it so I hope it's not a cracked head or something like that.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gold87SE||12-18-2000||01:36 PM||||Part of the problem is air getting into the system. We did a pressure test and there were no leaks. Could that be from a bad radiator cap?||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||12-18-2000||04:41 PM||||Try to track down where the noise is coming from. It's probably pressure being release. A new cap is cheap insurance. HOWEVER, two things led me to suspect a blown head gasket or a cracked head. 1) It's overheating and 2) you're over-pressurizing the coolant system.<P>Sometimes a cracked head will allow combustion gases to to forced into the coolant system. Two ways to check it, get some of that dye stuff that you pour into the radiator. It changes color if compustion gases are present. Also, sometimes a compression check will point out a problem.||||reg||1
